2.22.m:
"Oṃ bhūḥ brahma hṛdayāya; oṃ bhuvaḥ viṣṇuśirase; oṃ svaḥ rudraśikhāyai; oṃ bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ jvālāmālinīśikhāyai; oṃ mahaḥ maheśvarāya kavacāya; oṃ janaḥ śivāya netrebhyaḥ;
oṃ tapaḥ tāpakāya astrāya phaṭ." Thus the various solar mantras are declared;
with these vessels, such as horns, he should consecrate himself.

proceeding along the path purified by the water offering and stepping on sandals,
he should perform the hand-placement and body-placement as before;
taking the sacred water according to rule and entering the place of worship,

2.23.m:
“Oṃ bhūḥ, oṃ bhuvaḥ, oṃ svaḥ, oṃ mahaḥ, oṃ janaḥ, oṃ tapaḥ, oṃ satyam, oṃ ṛtam, oṃ brahman”—the formula consisting of nine syllables is proclaimed as the Bāṣkala.
Because it does not decay in this world, it is called the syllable;
the syllable is declared to be truth, beginning with the praṇava and ending with “homage”.

one should contemplate the destroyer of mundane existence having the form of a pure lamp-flame;
and one should worship the god Sadāśiva in a liṅga or on an altar.

Thus ends the twenty-third chapter in the Uttarabhāga of the glorious Liṅgamahāpurāṇa.
